

1. Concept: What is the Nuclear Dynamics Layer?

You already have:

[ U_N = \Phi_N({P_i}, {N_j}, F_N, C_N) ]

The Nuclear Dynamics Equation answers:

Given the current unified nuclear state (U_N),how does the nucleus change over time?

Call it:

[ N_N = \Psi_N(U_N, {P_i}, {N_j}, F_N, C_N) ]

Where (N_N) is a change field over the nuclear manifold:

how nucleon positions/momenta update

how nuclear fields update

how collective structure evolves (e.g., deformation)

2. Math sketch

Let:

({P_i(t)}): proton states

({N_j(t)}): neutron states

(F_N(t)): nuclear fields

(C_N(t)): constraints

Then:

[ \frac{d}{dt} \begin{bmatrix} {P_i} \ {N_j} \ F_N \end{bmatrix} = N_N = \Psi_N(U_N, {P_i}, {N_j}, F_N, C_N) ]

We don’t need exact nuclear physics—this is your nuclear‑organism dynamics, not a textbook solver.

